it's been described as the worst blow to  wildlife in israel's history  this nature reserve in the north was  once a bird watchers dream  migratory cranes gather here from colder  climates before moving south to africa  but avian flu has turned it into a  graveyard  the scope of the current outbreak in  israel is massive  and we see that it it's not stopping in  the wild animals so it is worrisome and  i must say that with the attention of  the israeli government and other  countries to covet 19 we should not  neglect other public health hazards and  we must tackle  avian influence as well  the cleanup is taking time the area now  off limits to the public for fear of  contagion  at first about two weeks ago when they  discovered the disease about 50 to 100  dead cranes were identified here within  a few days the numbers reached thousands  and today they're about five thousand to  six thousand  israel is taking preemptive measures and  is called hundreds of thousands of  chickens the virus hasn't yet been  contained  and even though we're told the threat of  spreading to humans is low it remains a  possibility that needs to be taken  seriously we fear of the possibility  that the same person will get infected  from human influenza  and from evian influenza and there will  be genetic reassortment and we will have  a new virus that is both  pathogenic  and easily spread among humans that's a  theoretical possibility but we must be  very alert in order to prevent it at a  time when the world is already  struggling to contain mutations of  covert 19 with omicron infections here  expected to double and triple over the  next few weeks  images like this  are only making people more nervous  stephanie decker al-jazeera jerusalem
 

